=== Introduction
--- Beautiful for Situation, Mount Zion [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 48
sub: The city of the great King • Zion and the promise • The kings marvelled and hasted away
refs: Psalm 48:1-2; Psalm 48:14
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Mount Zion
--- The city of the great King
kicker: Psalm 48 · Mount Zion
scripture: Psalm 48:1-2 | mark: Beautiful for situation, the joy of the whole earth, is mount Zion
p: Mount Zion, Jerusalem, is often referenced and praised as the place God chose to place His name. His holy city, the mountain providing natural protection on 3 sides, situated perfectly. A river running through the fertile valley. The setting where Abraham brought Isaac. Where God prepared Himself a sacrifice. Where Jesus was slain and rose again, and where He will reign in the Kingdom to come.
p: The name, Zion, means a “monument; raised up; sepulcher,” the place of the tomb, hewn out of rock, the site of Christ's burial. Chosen by God.

--- As the mountains are round about Jerusalem
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 48:2
p: Jerusalem, the city isn't a lonely peak, but rather a settlement nestled within a protective bowl of higher terrain, with Mt Moriah and the mount of Olives part of the limestone rings, defending the area.
scripture: Psalm 125:1-2 | mark: As the mountains are round about Jerusalem, so the LORD is round about his people
p: Beautiful for situation, a perfect location, the city of the great King on its north face.

--- The LORD hath chosen Zion
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 48:2
scripture: Psalm 132:13-14 | mark: For the LORD hath chosen Zion
scripture: 1 Peter 2:5-6 | mark: Behold, I lay in Sion a chief corner stone

--- A Lamb stood on the mount Sion
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 48:2
p: The shelter of Zion, the cornerstone of which is Christ, is where Jesus returns to in the end times, with the 144,000 witnesses.
big: Revelation 14:1 | mark: a Lamb stood on the mount Sion

--- Sennacherib
kicker: Psalm 48 · The defense of Zion
p: The defenses of this holy mountain were enough to dissuade kings and armies.
p: Sennacherib, of Assyria, the most powerful emperor of the ancient world, invaded Judah with a massive army of 185,000 soldiers.
p: He sent his top military commanders to the walls of Jerusalem, fiercely mocking King Hezekiah and blaspheming God, confidently expecting the city to fall just like every other nation before it.
p: Hezekiah called out to the Lord for protection of the city.

--- Hezekiah's prayer
kicker: Cross references • 2 Kings 19
scripture: 2 Kings 19:16-19 | mark: save thou us out of his hand, that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that thou art the LORD God

--- For mine own sake
kicker: Cross references • 2 Kings 19
p: And God harkened to the prayer, for His own purposes and will;
scripture: 2 Kings 19:32-34 | mark: For I will defend this city, to save it, for mine own sake, and for my servant David’s sake

--- Out of Sion the Deliverer
kicker: Psalm 48 · The holy city
p: Zion is defended for His own sake, because He placed His name there, and for the promise made to David that out of Zion shall come a King.
scripture: Romans 11:26-27 | mark: There shall come out of Sion the Deliverer
p: Zion is holy, because it has been separated unto the purposes of God.

--- Spiritually called Sodom and Egypt
kicker: Psalm 48 · Not holy for the people's sake
p: It is not holy because the people there are holy. God allowed Babylon to raze the city and remove the people. Jesus decreed that Jerusalem would fall, and in 70AD it did. With the end times approaching, the city is considered wicked;
big: Revelation 11:8 | mark: which spiritually is called Sodom and Egypt
p: As Christ said;
scripture: Matthew 23:37-38 | mark: Behold, your house is left unto you desolate

--- They build up Zion with blood
kicker: Psalm 48 · Not holy for the people's sake
p: The Jews cursed the land, building up Zion with blood of the righteous;
scripture: Micah 3:9-10 | mark: They build up Zion with blood
scripture: Lamentations 4:11-13 | mark: For the sins of her prophets, and the iniquities of her priests

=== The bride
--- A bride adorneth herself
kicker: Psalm 48 · Zion redeemed
p: But, Zion, like believers, stained crimson in sin, has a redeemer. And, like His church, Jerusalem is His bride, made perfect and decked in the finest array, fit for the King.
big: Isaiah 61:10 | mark: as a bride adorneth herself with her jewels

--- For Zion's sake
kicker: Cross references • Isaiah 62
scripture: Isaiah 62:1-3 | mark: For Zion’s sake will I not hold my peace

--- As the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride
kicker: Cross references • Isaiah 62
scripture: Isaiah 62:4-7 | mark: as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee

--- Jealous for Jerusalem
kicker: Psalm 48 · A picture of the believer
p: Zion, and Jerusalem, are a picture of the believer. A bride of Christ, made precious by His redemption, protecting it like a jealous husband;
big: Zechariah 1:14 | mark: I am jealous for Jerusalem and for Zion with a great jealousy
scripture: Isaiah 54:5-7 | mark: For thy Maker is thine husband

--- New Jerusalem
kicker: Psalm 48 · The bride of Christ
p: In the end, we shall see New Jerusalem, upon his holy hill, Zion. Perfected as a picture of his people, His bride;
scripture: Revelation 21:1-2 | mark: prepared as a bride adorned for her husband
p: This is the promise of his holy place, how much more so is it the promise for those who have trusted in Christ. In an unmovable Rock, the cornerstone laid in Zion. The Rock of our salvation, we who are the bride of Christ. Amen

=== Abraham and his seed
--- The promise to Abraham
kicker: Psalm 48 · The holy land
p: **Psalm 48** broadly captures the grandeur and love of God towards His holy land. This love is seen long before Israel as a nation enters in, or even exists. It was in the beginning, when God promised a land to Abraham.
scripture: Genesis 12:1-3 | mark: unto a land that I will shew thee; in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed

--- And to thy seed, which is Christ
kicker: Psalm 48 · The holy land
p: The promise to Abraham was both for the land, and the Christ. Long before the exodus. Long before the wandering. Before Isaac was ever born, the land that would become Israel, the province of Judah, the city of Jerusalem, in the mountains of Zion, was set apart for God.
p: The blessings and inheritance made to Abraham, who believed God and it was counted for righteousness, was made to him and his seed. Jesus Christ.
big: Galatians 3:16 | mark: And to thy seed, which is Christ

--- Zionism
kicker: Application • Zionism
p: Zionism is a modern political ideology that takes the promise to Abraham, and rather than give the inheritance and blessing to Christ, and all who are in Christ, gives it to unbelieving, apostate Jews.
scripture: Galatians 3:27-29 | mark: And if ye be Christ’s, then are ye Abraham’s seed

--- All came to pass
kicker: Cross references • The land
p: The claim for the children of Israel to the land, by promise of God, was fulfilled in the time of Joshua.
scripture: Joshua 21:43-45 | mark: There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken unto the house of Israel; all came to pass

--- Plucked from off the land
kicker: Cross references • The land
p: The promise to Abraham was unconditional. But the promise made to Moses by God in the wilderness, for His people to occupy His land, was conditional.
scripture: Deuteronomy 28:63-64 | mark: ye shall be plucked from off the land

--- Cannot disannul
kicker: Application • Zionism
p: Many Christians today will say "God keeps His promises! The Jews deserve this land!" They place political Zionism and the ethnic ultranationalism of the modern Jewish state as being part of an unconditional and irrevocable covenant with the people by heritage and genealogy.
p: But the Bible is clear, that the covenant made in the wilderness was explicitly conditional, that what was given to the people through Moses could not, by their failure, disannul the promise made to Abraham and his singular seed.
scripture: Galatians 3:17-18 | mark: God gave it to Abraham by promise

--- A new covenant
kicker: Cross references • The covenant
p: But the covenant made those 430 years after Abraham? It has faded away, replaced by a better covenant with better promises, to all who are in Christ, regardless of their ethnicity or origin.
scripture: Hebrews 8:7-10 | mark: I will make a new covenant

--- Joint-heirs with Christ
kicker: Cross references • The inheritance
p: The promise to occupy the land will be fulfilled for the Old Testament believers, in the resurrection. The only "Zionism" that matters is Christ Jesus on His throne. The inheritance is passed to all who have put on Christ.
scripture: Romans 8:14-17 | mark: heirs of God, and joint-heirs with Christ
p: This promise, the inheritance and blessings, is the heritage of all believers, from Adam and Abel, Seth and Noah, through every professing Christian today.

--- The spirit of Esau
kicker: Application • The birthright
p: The spirit of Esau, who forsook his inheritance for a bowl of pottage, lives in Christians, who for political expediency seek to give it away.
scripture: Hebrews 12:14-17 | mark: as Esau, who for one morsel of meat sold his birthright
p: This tearful repentance will be the case for many in the end times. Those who placed modern politics and bad theology above the joy unspeakable found in Jesus for all who believe, Jew or Gentile. Man or woman. Bond or free.
note: This first half of this **Psalm 48** study is really a work of love, and allowed me to lay out a positive, biblical understanding of Zion and Zionism, that's not simply countering a Dispensationalist's claims.

=== The kings marvelled
--- They hasted away
kicker: Psalm 48 · God is known for a refuge
scripture: Psalm 48:3-6 | mark: They saw it, and so they marvelled; they were troubled, and hasted away
p: This promise of God, to be a ready shelter for His people and to cause their enemies to marvel and fear His power, and flee, is demonstrated multiple times in scripture.
p: During the entry and conquest of the promised land, we see it happen to the 5 Kings and their armies.
big: Joshua 10:5 | mark: the five kings of the Amorites

--- Fear them not
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 48:4–5
p: God promises Joshua that He will win them the victory.
big: Joshua 10:8 | mark: Fear them not
p: And God kept His promise.
big: Joshua 10:10 | mark: And the LORD discomfited them before Israel

--- The strong hold of Zion
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 48:4–5
p: Ironically, despite the defeat of the King of Jerusalem, among all the others, Joshua was not able to conquer Jerusalem itself. This honor would fall on King David himself.
scripture: 2 Samuel 5:6-7 | mark: Nevertheless David took the strong hold of Zion
p: But this was not the only example of God confounding the enemies of the people, especially in the defense of Zion.

--- When Jehoshaphat was king
kicker: Psalm 48 · Ships of Tarshish
scripture: Psalm 48:7-8 | mark: God will establish it for ever
p: We've previously covered the attempted siege of Jerusalem by Sennacherib and the Assyrian Forces, from **2 Kings 19**, which is another example. But a more fitting instance occurred when Jehoshaphat was king.
p: A massive alliance of kings from Moab, Ammon, and Edom marched together to destroy Jerusalem.

--- Art not thou our God?
kicker: Cross references • 2 Chronicles 20
scripture: 2 Chronicles 20:3-7 | mark: Art not thou our God

--- The battle is not yours, but God's
kicker: Cross references • 2 Chronicles 20
p: As all Jerusalem prays, the Spirit of the Lord comes upon Jahaziel the son of Zechariah;
scripture: 2 Chronicles 20:15-17 | mark: for the battle is not yours, but God’s; stand ye still, and see the salvation of the LORD

--- We have thought of thy lovingkindness
kicker: Psalm 48 · Let mount Zion rejoice
scripture: Psalm 48:9-11 | mark: We have thought of thy lovingkindness, O God, in the midst of thy temple
p: The response by Jehoshaphat and the Levites, and all the people, was to trust in the Lord and sing His praises.
p: They thought of God's lovingkindness, they taught His word in the midst of the temple. They sang His praise to the ends of the earth. The mount of Zion rejoiced and Jerusalem's daughters were glad because of His judgements.

--- When they began to sing
kicker: Cross references • 2 Chronicles 20
scripture: 2 Chronicles 20:18-22 | mark: And when they began to sing and to praise, the LORD set ambushments

--- Our guide even unto death
kicker: Psalm 48 · Walk about Zion
p: The battle won by the enemy being discomfited and confounded. They turned their own swords upon each other. Smitten by the power of the Lord.
p: As promised, the spoil of the armies was delivered into the hands of Judah. And the people there praised His name.
scripture: Psalm 48:12-14 | mark: For this God is our God for ever and ever: he will be our guide even unto death
center: Amen

